# Splunk SPL — Data-Model Field Discovery Process

When you are about to author or edit SPL that reads from a Splunk
data model — i.e. any of the following command shapes:

- `| tstats <agg>(<DataSet>.<field>) ... from datamodel=<Model> ...`
- `| from datamodel:<Model>.<DataSet> ...`
- `| datamodel <Model> <DataSet> search`
- `| pivot <Model> <DataSet> ...`

you **must not guess** the dataset names, field names, or constraint
expressions. Splunk DMs use exact, case-sensitive dataset paths
(`Cisco_ThousandEyes.Test_Metrics`, `Authentication.Authentication`,
`Network_Traffic.All_Traffic`, etc.) and equally exact field names.
A typo (or a field that exists in raw events but isn't in the DM)
silently returns zero rows or null aggregates — the search still
"runs" but the dashboard is wrong.

## The required process — do this every time

Before writing or editing any DM-backed SPL, do **all** of the
following:

### 1. Identify which DM the search will use

Examples in this codebase:

| DM | Datasets used here |
|---|---|
| `Cisco_ThousandEyes` | `Test_Metrics`, `Alerts`, `Tests`, `Endpoints` |
| `Authentication` | `Authentication`, `Failed_Authentication` |
| `Network_Traffic` | `All_Traffic` |
| `cisco:dnac:devicehealth` (raw, no DM) | n/a — not all sourcetypes have a DM |
| `cisco:sdwan:linkhealth` (raw, no DM) | n/a |

If the data does not have a DM, fall back to direct
`<index_macro> sourcetype="..." ...` SPL and use the appropriate
field-list discovery process (run `<base> | `field_list`` and ask
the user to paste the output).

### 2. Ask the user for the DM JSON file

The authoritative source of truth for any DM is its JSON
definition shipped by the add-on that publishes the DM. Examples:

- Cisco ThousandEyes:
  `/opt/splunk/etc/apps/ta_cisco_thousandeyes/default/data/models/Cisco_ThousandEyes.json`
- Splunk CIM Authentication:
  `/opt/splunk/etc/apps/Splunk_SA_CIM/default/data/models/Authentication.json`
- Splunk CIM Network Traffic:
  `/opt/splunk/etc/apps/Splunk_SA_CIM/default/data/models/Network_Traffic.json`

The user may have a non-standard install path. **Ask** them to
provide the file path or paste its contents. Do not assume.

The required ask, verbatim:

> Before I write SPL against the `<Model>` data model, I need the
> JSON definition that ships with the add-on. By default it lives at
> `/opt/splunk/etc/apps/<ta_app>/default/data/models/<Model>.json`,
> but please confirm the path on your stack (or paste the file)
> so I can use the exact dataset and field names.

### 3. Read the JSON before writing SPL

Once the user provides the file or its contents, parse the
`objects` array. Each object is a dataset with:

- `objectName` — the dataset name you put after the model
  (`Cisco_ThousandEyes.<objectName>`).
- `fields[]` — the fields in that dataset, with:
  - `fieldName` — the exact name to put after `<DataSet>.` in
    `tstats` aggregate calls.
  - `type` — `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `timestamp`, `objectCount`.
  - `multivalue`, `required`, `hidden`.
- `calculations[]` — eval / lookup-derived fields. Pay attention:
  these may exist in the DM but not in raw events (or vice versa).
- `constraints[]` — the dataset's base search. If your filter
  duplicates a constraint, you're paying twice.

### 4. Build the SPL using exact names

For the metric store (e.g. ThousandEyes `cisco:thousandeyes:metric`)
the field names in the DM include the `metric_name:` prefix:

```
| tstats avg(Test_Metrics.metric_name:network.latency) as avg_latency_s
  from datamodel=Cisco_ThousandEyes
  where Test_Metrics.thousandeyes.test.type="agent-to-agent"
  by Test_Metrics.thousandeyes.source.agent.name,
     Test_Metrics.thousandeyes.target.agent.name
```

For raw-event-backed datasets, fields are named without the
`metric_name:` prefix and dotted-field quoting follows
`splunk-spl-field-syntax.mdc` (single-quote in `eval` / `where`
expressions, bare in `tstats` / `stats` argument and `by` lists).

### 5. Document the source you used

In any new DM-backed stanza's `description` or `explanation`,
mention the DM and dataset:

```
description = ... runs over the <code>Cisco_ThousandEyes.Test_Metrics</code>
data model, joining on <code>thousandeyes.test.type</code> ...
```

This makes the SPL's data-source assumption auditable later.

## What NOT to do

- ❌ Guess at field names ("it's probably `Test_Metrics.network.jitter`").
- ❌ Copy a field name from a raw-event search and paste it after a
  DM dataset prefix without confirming the DM accelerates that
  field.
- ❌ Mix raw-event field names and DM field names in the same
  search.
- ❌ Strip the `metric_name:` prefix from a metric-store DM field
  name. The DM JSON is the authority — if the field is
  `metric_name:network.latency` in the JSON, use exactly that.
- ❌ Quote DM field paths in `tstats` / `stats` argument or `by`
  lists. They follow the same bare-field rule as raw fields
  (see `splunk-spl-field-syntax.mdc`).

## Why this rule exists

This rule exists because the agent (me) has, repeatedly, written
SPL against the Cisco ThousandEyes DM that:

1. Used `Test_Metrics.network.jitter` instead of
   `Test_Metrics.metric_name:network.jitter` — silently null on
   the metric-store-backed dataset.
2. Used dataset names that don't exist (e.g. `Network` instead of
   `Test_Metrics`) — runs without error, returns zero rows.
3. Single-quoted DM field paths in `tstats` arguments — same silent-null
   failure mode.

Each of those bugs would have been caught immediately by reading
the DM JSON first. The user's standing rule is: **"I prefer to
use the DM to stay consistent. If you're using a DM, ask me for
the JSON."**

## Verification before committing DM-backed SPL

Before declaring a DM-backed SPL change ready:

1. Confirm every `<DataSet>.<field>` reference exists in the DM
   JSON's `objects[].fields[]` array.
2. Run the search against the user's stack and confirm non-null
   results (or, when the user can run the search, ask them to).
3. If using the metric store, confirm every metric field uses
   the `metric_name:` prefix.

You must always explain how this rule was applied and why it was
applied.
